<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tanit</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tanit was the chief goddess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and the Punic world, often paired with the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal-hammon">Baal Hammon</a> and called 'face of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>'. Her emblem, a triangle topped by a horizontal bar and a disc, appears on thousands of stelae, coins and mosaics from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and Ibiza. She was associated with fertility and protection, and under Roman rule her cult continued as that of Caelestis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanit.jpg" alt="Tanit" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArchaiOptix · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carthago_-_350-250_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Tanit_-_head_of_horse_and_date_palm_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
